  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The most transferable direct human F records for polar acidic/amide analogs all exceed the 20% threshold: tranexamic acid 33.4% (C21), gabapentin 37.7% (C28), penicillin V 54.8% in normal children (C24), captopril ~60% (C05), biotin ~100% (C09), and alpha-lipoic acid ~30% (C03).",
      "card_ids": ["C21","C28","C24","C05","C09","C03"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Direct human F for the cyclohexyl-containing imide/amide praziquantel in cirrhosis is 27.2% (C08), above threshold, despite extensive CYP3A4-mediated hepatic metabolism (C238, C239, C231, C223, C234, C253, C215); high hepatic clearance alone does not establish F <20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C08","C238","C239","C231","C223","C234","C253","C215"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Low in vitro permeability does not by itself establish F <20%: captopril Caco-2 Papp 0.07×10^-6 cm/s (C140) yet human F ~60% (C05); gabapentin PAMPA 0.02–0.026×10^-6 cm/s (C122, C132) yet F 37.7% (C28); penicillin V hPepT1-dependent permeability (C109, C153, C150) yet F 54.8% (C24). PEPT1-mediated uptake is documented for peptide-like acids (captopril C194, enalapril C158, ceftibuten C179).",
      "card_ids": ["C140","C122","C132","C109","C153","C150","C05","C28","C24","C194","C158","C179"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic-clearance records do not provide affirmative query-specific first-pass loss: ximelagatran/melagatran (polar peptide-like, cyclohexyl) show no CYP metabolism (C237, C249, C246, C211); acidic cyclohexyl sulfonylureas acetohexamide/glipizide show low CLint (C244, C250, C236, C241); piracetam shows low CLint (C251, C218, C256). No query-specific hepatic-clearance measurement exists, so this is an evidence gap, not evidence for F <20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C237","C249","C246","C211","C244","C250","C236","C241","C251","C218","C256"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C237","C249","C246","C211","C244","C250","C236","C241","C251","C218","C256","C238","C239","C231","C223","C234","C253","C215"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule.",
    "No query-specific Caco-2/PAMPA/MDCK permeability or transporter (PEPT1/OATP/efflux) data are available; L4/L5 records are for other molecules.",
    "No query-specific hepatic clearance or metabolic-stability data are available; L6 records are for other molecules and have low transfer likelihood.",
    "Unknown whether the dicyclohexyl barbiturate glycine amide is a substrate for intestinal uptake transporters or undergoes significant gut-wall efflux/first-pass metabolism.",
    "L3 exposure records lack matched IV comparators, so they cannot quantify F.",
    "Species differences, such as penicillin V 18.9% in pigs versus 54.8% in children, limit extrapolation."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Keep high: the most transferable direct human F records for polar acidic/amide analogs all exceed 20%, and L4/L5 show that low in vitro permeability is common among such compounds with F >20% and can be overcome by PEPT1-type uptake (captopril, gabapentin, penicillin V, ceftibuten, enalapril). L6 adds no query-specific hepatic-clearance evidence of presystemic loss; low-clearance records for polar/acidic cyclohexyl compounds are consistent with limited first-pass metabolism, while the high-clearance praziquantel example is structurally distant and its F in cirrhosis is above threshold. No affirmative threshold-relevant evidence supports F <20%; confidence remains low because no query-specific F, transporter, or clearance data exist and the query is larger and more polar than the closest analogs."
}

